{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 49, :comments ["<b>Only the tribe of Levi you shall not number, etc.</b> If he is not to number them, it is obvious that he would not receive the sum of their count; so what does the verse teach by saying 'and their heads you shall not lift among the children of Israel,' and what would the verse have lacked by saying only 'Only the tribe of Levi you shall not number among the children of Israel'? And according to what Rashi explained nearby, that the counting was by command of HaShem — for the Shekhinah would go before Him and a bat kol would issue from the Tent and say: So-and-so many infants are in the Tent — we may say that 'among the children of Israel' as stated is attached only to 'and their heads you shall not lift,' as if it said: Only the tribe of Levi you shall not number at all, not even among the children of Israel. For every אַךְ comes to exclude, so too here it comes to exclude and to say: you alone shall not number them, but in conjunction with the Shekhinah and the bat kol they shall be numbered. Therefore it says: 'Number the children of Levi' — that is, by means of the Shekhinah; but you alone shall not number them. And from the fact that right after this comes the command 'And you, appoint the Levites over the tabernacle of the testimony,' it appears that everywhere the verb שׂאוּ, נָשֹׂא, כִּי תִשָּׂא is used, it all means elevation and headship of rank that is visible in those who are counted through their superiority over others, for every important and counted thing has a number." "And with this I find it possible to settle many variations, for regarding Israel two expressions were used: 'Lift the heads' and afterward it says 'you shall number them by their hosts'; but regarding the Levites he mentioned only the expression of counting — 'Number the children of Levi'; and regarding the firstborns he went back and mentioned both: 'Number every firstborn,' finishing, 'and lift the sum of their names'; and regarding the children of Kehat and the children of Gershon he mentioned the expression 'נָשֹׂא' but not regarding the children of Merari. And this is because the פְּקִידָה is the counting, and 'שְׂאוּ' is the expression of elevation of rank, which Israel needed in order to show their belovedness against all the nations, who are likened to straw that has no number; but the Levites have no need to show their preeminence by means of a number, for even without that they have headship in that the Holy One, blessed be He, made them officers over His holy house. Therefore it says afterward 'and their heads you shall not lift,' etc. — 'And you, appoint the Levites,' to tell you that from the side of their having been made officers they have headship apart from the counting that is among the children of Israel, for they are the legion of the King, and all who see them will recognize them, that they are the seed that HaShem has blessed. But the firstborns, who were disqualified from the divine service on account of the sin of the golden calf, needed headship by means of the number; therefore he specified both expressions regarding them. And as for his writing the expression 'נָשֹׂא' regarding the children of Kehat and the children of Gershon, that is to give them headship over the children of Merari, for the service of both of them is more sacred than the burden of the children of Merari; therefore he did not mention 'נָשֹׂא' regarding the children of Merari."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:37.215132316Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}